1
00:00:00,359 --> 00:00:03,478
part 2 chapter 3.

2
00:00:03,478 --> 00:00:05,818
We can come here once again,' said Julia.

3
00:00:05,818 --> 00:00:12,478
'It's generally safe to use any Hideout twice. But not for another month or two, of course.'

4
00:00:12,478 --> 00:00:16,500
As soon as she woke up her demeanor had changed. She became alert and

5
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:20,879
business-like put her clothes on not at the scarlet sash about her waist and

6
00:00:20,879 --> 00:00:23,818
began arranging the details of the journey home.

7
00:00:23,818 --> 00:00:26,159
It seemed natural to leave this to her.

8
00:00:26,159 --> 00:00:30,359
She obviously had a practical cunning which Winston lacked, and she seemed also

9
00:00:30,359 --> 00:00:34,020
to have an exhaustive knowledge of the countryside around London, stored away

10
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:36,659
from innumerable community hikes.

11
00:00:36,659 --> 00:00:39,540
The route she gave him was quite different from the one by which he had

12
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:42,840
come, and brought him out at a different railway station.

13
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:47,159
'Never go home the same way as you went out,' she said, as though enunciating an

14
00:00:47,159 --> 00:00:49,079
important general principle.

15
00:00:49,079 --> 00:00:54,299
She would leave first, and Winston was to wait half an hour before following her.

16
00:00:54,299 --> 00:00:58,559
She had named a place where they could meet after work, four evenings hence.

17
00:00:58,559 --> 00:01:02,040
It was a street in one of the poorer quarters, where there was an open market

18
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:05,279
which was generally crowded and noisy.

19
00:01:05,279 --> 00:01:08,760
She would be hanging about among the stalls, pretending to be in search of

20
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:13,020
shoelaces or sewing thread If she judged that the coast was clear

21
00:01:13,020 --> 00:01:16,859
she would blow her nose when he approached: otherwise he was to walk past

22
00:01:16,859 --> 00:01:18,840
her without recognition.

23
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:22,260
But with luck, in the middle of the crowd, it would be safe to talk for a quarter

24
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:25,500
of an hour and arrange another meeting.

25
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:30,540
'And now I must go,' she said as soon as he had mastered his instructions. 'I'm due

26
00:01:30,540 --> 00:01:34,799
back at 1930. I've got to put in two hours for the Junior Anti-Sex League,

27
00:01:34,799 --> 00:01:37,200
handing out leaflets, or something.

28
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:38,818
Isn't it bloody?

29
00:01:38,818 --> 00:01:43,620
Give me a brush down would you. Have I got any twigs in my hair? Are you sure?

30
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:49,260
Then goodbye my love goodbye flung herself into his arms, kissed him

31
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:53,398
almost violently, and a moment later pushed her way through the saplings and

32
00:01:53,398 --> 00:01:57,420
disappeared into the wood with very little noise. Even now he had not found

33
00:01:57,420 --> 00:01:59,639
out her surname or her address.

34
00:01:59,639 --> 00:02:03,600
However, it made no difference, for it was inconceivable that they could ever meet

35
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:07,500
indoors or exchange any kind of written communication.

36
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:11,159
As it happened they never went back to the clearing in the wood.

37
00:02:11,159 --> 00:02:14,398
During the month of May there was only one further occasion on which they

38
00:02:14,398 --> 00:02:16,859
actually succeeded in making love.

39
00:02:16,859 --> 00:02:21,359
That was in another Hiding Place known to Julia, the belfry of a ruinous church

40
00:02:21,359 --> 00:02:26,639
in an almost deserted stretch of country where an atomic bomb had fallen 30 years earlier.

41
00:02:26,639 --> 00:02:31,559
It was a good hiding place when once you got there, but the getting there was very dangerous.

42
00:02:31,559 --> 00:02:35,340
For the rest they could meet only in the streets, in a different place every

43
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:38,459
evening and never for more than half an hour at a time.

44
00:02:38,459 --> 00:02:42,238
In the street it was usually possible to talk, after a fashion.

45
00:02:42,238 --> 00:02:45,719
As they drifted down the crowded pavements, not quite abreast and never

46
00:02:45,719 --> 00:02:50,040
looking at one another, they carried on a curious, intermittent conversation which

47
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:53,818
flicked on and off like the beams of a lighthouse, suddenly nipped into silence

48
00:02:53,818 --> 00:02:58,379
by the approach of a Party uniform or the proximity of a telescreen, then taken

49
00:02:58,379 --> 00:03:02,159
up again minutes later in the middle of a sentence, then abruptly cut short as

50
00:03:02,159 --> 00:03:07,859
they parted at the agreed spot, then continued almost without introduction on the following day.

51
00:03:07,859 --> 00:03:11,579
Julia appeared to be quite used to this kind of conversation, which he called

52
00:03:11,579 --> 00:03:16,139
'talking by installments She was also surprisingly adept at

53
00:03:16,139 --> 00:03:19,978
speaking without moving her lips. Just once in almost a month of nightly

54
00:03:19,978 --> 00:03:22,799
meetings they managed to exchange a kiss.

55
00:03:22,799 --> 00:03:27,000
They were passing in silence down a side street Julia would never speak when they

56
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,600
were away from the main streets) when there was a deafening roar, the earth

57
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:35,279
heaved and the air darkened, and Winston found himself lying on his side, bruised

58
00:03:35,279 --> 00:03:39,840
and terrified. A rocket bomb must have dropped quite near at hand.

59
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:44,219
Suddenly he became aware of Julia's face a few centimeters from his own, deathly

60
00:03:44,219 --> 00:03:47,818
white, as white as chalk. Even her lips were white.

61
00:03:47,818 --> 00:03:53,398
She was dead! He clasped her against him and found that he was kissing a live warm face.

62
00:03:53,398 --> 00:03:56,760
But there was some powdery stuff that got in the way of his lips.

63
00:03:56,760 --> 00:04:01,279
Both of their faces were thickly coated with plaster.

64
00:04:01,379 --> 00:04:04,500
There were evenings when they reached their rendezvous and then had to walk

65
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:08,520
past one another without a sign, because a patrol had just come around the corner

66
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:11,040
or a helicopter was hovering overhead.

67
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:16,620
Even if it had been less dangerous, it would still have been difficult to find time to meet.

68
00:04:16,620 --> 00:04:20,818
Winston's working week was 60 hours Julius was even longer, and their free

69
00:04:20,818 --> 00:04:24,719
days varied according to the pressure of work and did not often coincide.

70
00:04:24,719 --> 00:04:28,799
Julia, in any case, seldom had an evening completely free.

71
00:04:28,799 --> 00:04:32,600
She spent an astonishing amount of time in attending lectures and demonstrations,

72
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:36,898
distributing literature for the Junior Anti-Sex League, preparing banners for

73
00:04:36,898 --> 00:04:41,760
Hate Week, making collections for the savings campaign, and such like activities

74
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:46,020
page he said; it was camouflage. If you kept the small rules you could break the

75
00:04:46,020 --> 00:04:50,398
big ones. She even induced Winston to mortgage yet another of his evenings by

76
00:04:50,398 --> 00:04:53,818
enrolling himself for the part-time munition work which was done voluntarily

77
00:04:53,818 --> 00:04:56,040
by zealous Party members.

78
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:00,359
So, one evening every week, Winston spent four hours of paralyzing boredom,

79
00:05:00,359 --> 00:05:04,439
screwing together small bits of metal which were probably parts of bomb fuses,

80
00:05:04,439 --> 00:05:08,699
in a drafty ill-lit Workshop where the knocking of hammers mingled drearily

81
00:05:08,699 --> 00:05:11,520
with the music of the telescreens.

82
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:15,359
When they met in the church tower the gaps in their fragmentary conversation

83
00:05:15,359 --> 00:05:20,100
were filled up. It was a blazing afternoon. The air in the little square

84
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:25,500
chamber above the bells was hot and stagnant, and smelt overpoweringly of pigeon dung

85
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:29,939
They sat talking for hours on the dusty twig-litted floor, one or other of them

86
00:05:29,939 --> 00:05:33,600
getting up from time to time to cast a glance through the arrow slits and to

87
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:36,478
make sure that no one was coming.

88
00:05:36,478 --> 00:05:42,059
Julia was 26 years old. She lived in a hostel with 30 other girls ('Always in the

89
00:05:42,059 --> 00:05:45,478
stink of women! How I hate women!' she said parenthetically),

90
00:05:45,478 --> 00:05:50,520
and she worked, as he had guessed, on the novel writing machines in the Fiction Department.

91
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:54,120
She enjoyed her work, which consisted chiefly in running and servicing a

92
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:56,398
powerful but tricky electric motor.

93
00:05:56,398 --> 00:06:01,680
She was 'not clever', but was fond of using her hands and felt at home with machinery.

94
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:05,219
She could describe the whole process of composing a novel, from the general

95
00:06:05,219 --> 00:06:08,579
directive issued by the Planning Committee down to the final touching up

96
00:06:08,579 --> 00:06:12,959
by the Rewrite Squad. But she was not interested in the finished product.

97
00:06:12,959 --> 00:06:15,959
She 'didn't much care for reading', she said.

98
00:06:15,959 --> 00:06:21,120
Books were just a commodity that had to be produced, like jam or boot laces

99
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:25,620
She had no memories of anything before the early 60s and the only person she

100
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:29,398
had ever known who talked frequently of the days before the Revolution was a

101
00:06:29,398 --> 00:06:33,120
grandfather who had disappeared when she was eight. At school she had been captain

102
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:37,139
of the hockey team and had won the gymnastics trophy two years running.

103
00:06:37,139 --> 00:06:40,680
She had been a troop leader in the Spies and a branch secretary in the Youth

104
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:43,620
League before joining the Junior Anti-Sex League.

105
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:47,939
She had always born an excellent character. She had even (an infallible

106
00:06:47,939 --> 00:06:52,139
mark of good reputation) been picked out to work in pornosek

107
00:06:52,139 --> 00:06:55,799
the subsection of the Fiction Department which turned out cheap pornography for

108
00:06:55,799 --> 00:06:58,139
distribution among the proles.

109
00:06:58,139 --> 00:07:02,040
It was nicknamed Muck House by the people who worked in it, she remarked.

110
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:06,059
There she had remained for a year, helping to produce booklets in sealed

111
00:07:06,059 --> 00:07:10,139
packets with titles like Spanking Stories or One Night in a Girls' School,

112
00:07:10,139 --> 00:07:14,279
to be bought furtively by proletarian youths who were under the impression

113
00:07:14,279 --> 00:07:17,279
that they were buying something illegal.

114
00:07:17,279 --> 00:07:22,500
'What are these books like?' said Winston curiously. 'Oh, ghastly rubbish. They're

115
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:26,579
boring, really. They only have six plots, but they swap them around a bit.

116
00:07:26,579 --> 00:07:29,760
Of course I was only on the kaleidoscopes. I was never in the Rewrite

117
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:34,620
Squad. I'm not literary, dear— —not even enough for that.'

118
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:38,219
He learned with astonishment that all the workers in ponosec except the heads

119
00:07:38,219 --> 00:07:40,199
of the departments were girls.

120
00:07:40,199 --> 00:07:43,620
The theory was that men, whose sex instincts were less controllable than

121
00:07:43,620 --> 00:07:48,779
those of women, were in greater danger of being corrupted by the filth they handled.

122
00:07:48,779 --> 00:07:53,279
'They don't even like having married women there,' she added. 'Girls are always

123
00:07:53,279 --> 00:07:55,079
supposed to be so pure.

124
00:07:55,079 --> 00:07:57,719
Here's one who isn't, anyway.'

125
00:07:57,719 --> 00:08:02,219
She had had her first love affair when she was 16 with a Party member of 60 who

126
00:08:02,219 --> 00:08:05,159
later committed suicide to avoid arrest.

127
00:08:05,159 --> 00:08:09,420
'And a good job too,' said Julia, 'otherwise they'd have had my name out of him when

128
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:10,978
he confessed.'

129
00:08:10,978 --> 00:08:13,500
Since then there had been various others.

130
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:18,659
Life as she saw it was quite simple. You wanted a good time; 'they', meaning the

131
00:08:18,659 --> 00:08:22,978
Party, wanted to stop you having it; you broke the rules as best you could. She

132
00:08:22,978 --> 00:08:26,100
seemed to think it just as natural that 'they' should want to rob you of your

133
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:29,279
pleasures is that you should want to avoid being caught.

134
00:08:29,279 --> 00:08:33,600
She hated the Party, and said so in the crudest words, but she made no general

135
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:35,159
criticism of it.

136
00:08:35,159 --> 00:08:39,600
Except where it touched upon her own life she had no interest in Party doctrine.

137
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:43,439
He noticed that she never used Newspeak words, except the ones that had passed

138
00:08:43,439 --> 00:08:47,818
into everyday use. She had never heard of the Brotherhood, and refused to believe

139
00:08:47,818 --> 00:08:49,439
in its existence.

140
00:08:49,439 --> 00:08:53,579
Any kind of organized revolt against the Party, which was bound to be a failure,

141
00:08:53,579 --> 00:08:55,379
struck her as stupid.

142
00:08:55,379 --> 00:08:59,159
The clever thing was to break the rules and stay alive all the same.

143
00:08:59,159 --> 00:09:02,459
He wondered vaguely how many others like her there might be in the younger

144
00:09:02,459 --> 00:09:06,000
generation— —people who had grown up in the world of the Revolution, knowing

145
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,260
nothing else, accepting the Party as something unalterable, like the sky, not

146
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:15,559
rebelling against its authority but simply evading it, as a rabbit dodges a dog.

147
00:09:15,559 --> 00:09:20,159
They did not discuss the possibility of getting married. It was too remote to be

148
00:09:20,159 --> 00:09:21,540
worth thinking about.

149
00:09:21,540 --> 00:09:24,719
No imaginable committee would ever sanction such a marriage even if

150
00:09:24,719 --> 00:09:28,318
Catherine Winston's wife, could somehow have been got rid of.

151
00:09:28,318 --> 00:09:31,620
It was hopeless even as a daydream.

152
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:34,738
'What was she like, your wife?' said Julia.

153
00:09:34,738 --> 00:09:41,040
'She was— —do you know the new speak word good thinkful Meaning naturally orthodox, incapable of

154
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:43,199
thinking a bad thought?'

155
00:09:43,199 --> 00:09:47,818
'No, I didn't know the word, but I know the kind of person, right enough.'

156
00:09:47,818 --> 00:09:52,020
He began telling her the story of his married life, but curiously enough she

157
00:09:52,020 --> 00:09:54,719
appeared to know the essential parts of it already.

158
00:09:54,719 --> 00:09:59,100
She described to him, almost as though she had seen or felt it, the stiffening

159
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:02,459
of Catherine's body as soon as he touched her, the way in which she still

160
00:10:02,459 --> 00:10:06,478
seemed to be pushing him from her with all her strength, even when her arms were

161
00:10:06,478 --> 00:10:08,939
clasped tightly around him.

162
00:10:08,939 --> 00:10:13,199
With Julia he felt no difficulty in talking about such things Catherine in

163
00:10:13,199 --> 00:10:18,719
any case, had long ceased to be a painful memory and became merely a distasteful one.

164
00:10:18,719 --> 00:10:23,219
'I could have stood it if it hadn't been for one thing,' he said. He told her about

165
00:10:23,219 --> 00:10:26,100
the frigid little ceremony that Catherine had forced him to go through

166
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:28,139
on the same night every week.

167
00:10:28,139 --> 00:10:32,879
'She hated it, but nothing would make her stop doing it. She used to call it— —but

168
00:10:32,879 --> 00:10:34,500
you'll never guess.'

169
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:37,859
'Our duty to the Party,' said Julia promptly.

170
00:10:37,859 --> 00:10:40,199
'How did you know that?'

171
00:10:40,199 --> 00:10:45,299
'I've been at school too, dear. Sex talks once a month for the over 16s And in the

172
00:10:45,299 --> 00:10:49,978
Youth Movement. They rub it into you for years. I dare say it works in a lot of

173
00:10:49,978 --> 00:10:54,420
cases. But of course you can never tell; people are such hypocrites.'

174
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:56,398
She began to enlarge upon the subject.

175
00:10:56,398 --> 00:11:00,238
With Julia, everything came back to her own sexuality.

176
00:11:00,238 --> 00:11:05,219
As soon as this was touched upon in any way she was capable of Greater cuteness

177
00:11:05,219 --> 00:11:09,359
Unlike Winston, she had grasped the inner meaning of the Party's sexual puritanism.

178
00:11:09,359 --> 00:11:13,920
It was not merely that the sex instinct created a world of its own which was

179
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:18,359
outside the Party's control and which therefore had to be destroyed if possible.

180
00:11:18,359 --> 00:11:22,738
What was more important was that sexual privation induced hysteria, which was

181
00:11:22,738 --> 00:11:26,760
desirable because it could be transformed into war fever and leader worship

182
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:30,420
The way she put it was: 'When you make love you're using up

183
00:11:30,420 --> 00:11:34,500
energy; and afterwards you feel happy and don't give a damn for anything.

184
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:36,299
They can't bear you to feel like that.

185
00:11:36,299 --> 00:11:39,478
They want you to be bursting with energy all the time.

186
00:11:39,478 --> 00:11:44,579
All this marching up and down and cheering and waving flags is simply sex gone sour.

187
00:11:44,579 --> 00:11:47,699
If you're happy inside yourself, why should you get excited about Big Brother

188
00:11:47,699 --> 00:11:53,059
and the Three-Year Plans and the Two Minutes Hate and all the rest of their bloody rot?'

189
00:11:53,059 --> 00:11:57,420
That was very true, he thought. There was a direct, intimate connection between

190
00:11:57,420 --> 00:12:01,978
chastity and political orthodoxy. For how could the fear, the hatred and the

191
00:12:01,978 --> 00:12:05,879
lunatic credulity which the Party needed in its members be kept at the right

192
00:12:05,879 --> 00:12:11,699
pitch, except by bottling down some powerful instinct and using it as a driving force?

193
00:12:11,699 --> 00:12:15,478
The sex impulse was dangerous to the Party, and the Party had turned into

194
00:12:15,478 --> 00:12:19,620
account. They had played a similar trick with the instinct of parenthood.

195
00:12:19,620 --> 00:12:22,620
The family could not actually be abolished, and, indeed, people were

196
00:12:22,620 --> 00:12:26,459
encouraged to be fond of their children in almost the old-fashioned way.

197
00:12:26,459 --> 00:12:29,340
The children, on the other hand, were systematically turned against their

198
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:33,238
parents and taught to spy on them and report their deviations.

199
00:12:33,238 --> 00:12:37,500
The family had become in effect an extension of the Thought Police. It was a

200
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:41,100
device by means of which everyone could be surrounded night and day by informers

201
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:44,000
who knew him intimately.

202
00:12:44,219 --> 00:12:48,299
Abruptly his mind went back to Catherine Catherine would unquestionably have

203
00:12:48,299 --> 00:12:52,139
denounced him to the Thought Police if she had not happened to be too stupid to

204
00:12:52,139 --> 00:12:54,959
detect the unorthodoxy of his opinions.

205
00:12:54,959 --> 00:12:58,559
But what really recalled her to him at this moment was the stifling heat of the

206
00:12:58,559 --> 00:13:02,818
afternoon, which had brought the sweat out on his forehead. He began telling

207
00:13:02,818 --> 00:13:06,719
Julia of something that had happened, or rather had failed to happen, on another

208
00:13:06,719 --> 00:13:10,318
sweltering summer afternoon 11 years ago.

209
00:13:10,318 --> 00:13:14,639
It was three or four months after they were married. They had lost their way on

210
00:13:14,639 --> 00:13:18,779
a community hike somewhere in Kent. They had only lagged behind the others for a

211
00:13:18,779 --> 00:13:22,020
couple of minutes, but they took a wrong turning, and presently found themselves

212
00:13:22,020 --> 00:13:25,680
pulled up short by the edge of an old chalk quarry.

213
00:13:25,680 --> 00:13:29,818
It was a sheer drop of 10 or 20 meters with boulders at the bottom. There was

214
00:13:29,818 --> 00:13:32,040
nobody of whom they could ask the way.

215
00:13:32,040 --> 00:13:36,120
As soon as she realized that they were lost Catherine became very uneasy.

216
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:40,379
To be away from the noisy mob of hikers even for a moment gave her a feeling of

217
00:13:40,379 --> 00:13:45,299
wrongdoing She wanted to hurry back by the way they had come and start searching in the

218
00:13:45,299 --> 00:13:46,500
other direction.

219
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:50,279
But at this moment Winston noticed some tufts of loose strife growing in the

220
00:13:50,279 --> 00:13:52,318
cracks of the cliff beneath them.

221
00:13:52,318 --> 00:13:57,540
One tuft was of two colors magenta and brick red apparently growing on the same route

222
00:13:57,540 --> 00:14:01,020
He had never seen anything of the kind before, and he called to Catherine to

223
00:14:01,020 --> 00:14:02,340
come and look at it.

224
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:04,020
'Look Catherine Look at those flowers.

225
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:08,219
That clumped down near the bottom. Do you see they're two different colors

226
00:14:08,219 --> 00:14:12,600
She had already turned to go, but she did rather fretfully come back for a moment.

227
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:16,859
She even leaned out over the cliff face to see where he was pointing. He was

228
00:14:16,859 --> 00:14:21,420
standing a little behind her, and he put his hand on her waist to steady her. At

229
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:24,840
this moment it suddenly occurred to him how completely alone they were.

230
00:14:24,840 --> 00:14:29,340
There was not a human creature anywhere, not a leaf stirring, not even a bird awake.

231
00:14:29,340 --> 00:14:32,579
In a place like this the danger that there would be a hidden microphone was

232
00:14:32,579 --> 00:14:36,238
very small, and even if there was a microphone it would only pick up sounds.

233
00:14:36,238 --> 00:14:40,020
It was the hottest, sleepiest hour of the afternoon.

234
00:14:40,020 --> 00:14:45,659
The sun blazed down upon them, the sweat tickled his face. And the thought struck him....

235
00:14:45,659 --> 00:14:47,579
'Why didn't you give her a good shove?'

236
00:14:47,579 --> 00:14:49,978
said Julia. 'I would have.'

237
00:14:49,978 --> 00:14:52,620
'Yes, dear, you would have. I would if I'd

238
00:14:52,620 --> 00:14:58,079
been the same person then as I am now. Or perhaps I would— —I'm not certain.'

239
00:14:58,079 --> 00:15:00,059
'Are you sorry you didn't?'

240
00:15:00,059 --> 00:15:03,600
'Yes. On the whole I'm sorry I didn't.'

241
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:06,359
They were sitting side by side on the dusty floor.

242
00:15:06,359 --> 00:15:10,680
He pulled her closer against him. Her head rested on his shoulder, the pleasant

243
00:15:10,680 --> 00:15:16,199
smell of her hair conquering the pigeon dung She was very young, he thought, she still

244
00:15:16,199 --> 00:15:20,159
expected something from life, she did not understand that to push an inconvenient

245
00:15:20,159 --> 00:15:23,459
person over a cliff solves nothing.

246
00:15:23,459 --> 00:15:26,340
'Actually it would have made no difference,' he said.

247
00:15:26,340 --> 00:15:29,100
'Then why are you sorry you didn't do it?'

248
00:15:29,100 --> 00:15:33,180
'Only because I prefer a positive to a negative. In this game that we're playing,

249
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:38,040
we can't win. Some kinds of failure are better than other kinds, that's all.'

250
00:15:38,040 --> 00:15:42,840
He felt her shoulders give a wriggle of descent She always contradicted him when he said

251
00:15:42,840 --> 00:15:44,398
anything of this kind.

252
00:15:44,398 --> 00:15:48,719
She would not accept it as a law of nature that the individual is always defeated.

253
00:15:48,719 --> 00:15:52,500
In a way she realized that she herself was doomed, that sooner or later the

254
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:55,920
Thought Police would catch her and kill her, but with another part of her mind

255
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:59,520
she believed that it was somehow possible to construct a secret world in

256
00:15:59,520 --> 00:16:01,620
which you could live as you chose.

257
00:16:01,620 --> 00:16:06,359
All you needed was luck and cunning and boldness. She did not understand that

258
00:16:06,359 --> 00:16:09,840
there was no such thing as happiness, that the only victory lay in the far

259
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:13,680
future, long after you were dead, that from the moment of declaring war on the

260
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:17,398
Party it was better to think of yourself as a corpse.

261
00:16:17,398 --> 00:16:19,799
'We are the dead,' he said.

262
00:16:19,799 --> 00:16:23,159
'We're not dead yet,' said Julia prosaically.

263
00:16:23,159 --> 00:16:29,040
'Not physically. Six months, a year— —five years, conceivably I'm afraid of death.

264
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:33,659
You were young, so presumably you're more afraid of it than I am. Obviously we

265
00:16:33,659 --> 00:16:37,738
should put it off as long as we can. But it makes very little difference. So long

266
00:16:37,738 --> 00:16:42,478
as human beings stay human, death and life are the same thing.'

267
00:16:42,478 --> 00:16:46,859
'Oh, rubbish! Which would you sooner sleep with, me or a skeleton?

268
00:16:46,859 --> 00:16:51,898
Don't you enjoy being alive? Don't you like feeling: This is me, this is my hand,

269
00:16:51,898 --> 00:16:54,840
this is my leg, I'm real, I'm solid, I'm alive!

270
00:16:54,840 --> 00:16:56,939
Don't you like this?''

271
00:16:56,939 --> 00:17:00,359
She twisted herself around and pressed her bosom against him.

272
00:17:00,359 --> 00:17:05,279
He could feel her breasts, ripe yet firm, through her overalls. Her body seemed to

273
00:17:05,279 --> 00:17:08,520
be pouring some of its youth and vigor into his.

274
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:11,279
'Yes, I like that,' he said.

275
00:17:11,279 --> 00:17:15,478
'Then stop talking about dying. And now listen to you we've got to fix up about

276
00:17:15,478 --> 00:17:19,500
the next time we meet. We may as well go back to the place in the wood. We've

277
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:23,100
given it a good long rest. But you must get there by a different way this time.

278
00:17:23,100 --> 00:17:28,859
I've got it all planned out. You take the train— —but look, I'll draw it out for you.'

279
00:17:28,859 --> 00:17:33,059
And in her practical way she scraped together a small square of dust, and with

280
00:17:33,059 --> 00:17:37,000
a twig from a pigeon's nest began drawing a map on the floor
